            {
                type: 'formula',
                title: 'Restoring Force',
                latex: 'F = -mg\\sin\\theta',
                context: 'We resolve the weight into components. The component perpendicular to the string acts as the restoring force, bringing the bob back to the mean position.',
                variables: [
                    { symbol: 'F', meaning: 'Restoring Force' },
                    { symbol: 'm', meaning: 'Mass of the bob' },
                    { symbol: 'g', meaning: 'Acceleration due to gravity' },
                    { symbol: '\\theta', meaning: 'Angular displacement' }
                ]
            },
            {
                type: 'formula',
                title: 'Small Angle Approximation',
                latex: '\\sin\\theta \\approx \\theta \\quad (\\text{if } \\theta \\text{ is small})',
                context: 'If the angle \(\theta\) is small (typically less than 15°), we can approximate \(\sin\theta\) as \(\theta\) (measured in radians). This linearizes the equation of motion.',
                variables: []
            },
            {
                type: 'formula',
                title: 'Equation of Motion',
                latex: 'F = ma \\Rightarrow -mg\\theta = m \\frac{d^2x}{dt^2}',
                context: 'Using Newton\'s second law. Since the arc length \(x = L\\theta\), the acceleration \(a = L \\frac{d^2\\theta}{dt^2}\). Substituting this gives the differential equation for SHM.',
                variables: []
            },
            {
                type: 'formula',
                title: 'Standard SHM Differential Equation',
                latex: '\\frac{d^2\\theta}{dt^2} + \\frac{g}{L}\\theta = 0',
                context: 'Comparing this with the standard SHM equation \(\\frac{d^2y}{dt^2} + \\omega^2 y = 0\), we can identify the angular frequency \(\omega\).',
                variables: []
            },
            {
                type: 'formula',
                title: 'Angular Frequency',
                latex: '\\omega = \\sqrt{\\frac{g}{L}}',
                context: 'From the comparison above, the square of the angular frequency is equal to \(g/L\).',
                variables: []
            },
            {
                type: 'formula',
                title: 'Time Period (Final Expression)',
                latex: 'T = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega} = 2\\pi \\sqrt{\\frac{L}{g}}',
                context: 'The time period \(T\) is the time taken for one complete oscillation. Since \(\omega = 2\\pi/T\), we substitute \(\omega\) to get the final formula. Note that T is independent of mass.',
                variables: [
                    { symbol: 'T', meaning: 'Time Period' },
                    { symbol: 'L', meaning: 'Length of the string' },
                    { symbol: 'g', meaning: 'Acceleration due to gravity' }
                ],
                isMain: true // Flag for the Formula Lab
            },
            {
                type: 'table',
                title: 'Variable Summary',
                headers: ['Symbol', 'Quantity', 'SI Unit'],
                rows: [
                    ['T', 'Time Period', 'second (s)'],
                    ['L', 'Length', 'meter (m)'],
                    ['g', 'Gravity', 'm/s²'],
                    ['m', 'Mass', 'kilogram (kg)']
                ]
