  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Introduction</div>
    <p>Waves are disturbances that transfer energy through a medium or space. They are broadly classified into two main types based on the direction of oscillation relative to the direction of energy transfer: transverse and longitudinal waves.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Definition</div>
    <p><b>Transverse Waves:</b> These are waves in which the displacement of the medium is perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation.  Think of it like a "side-to-side" movement.</p>
    <p><b>Longitudinal Waves:</b> In these waves, the displacement of the medium is parallel to the direction of wave propagation.  Think of it like a "back-and-forth" movement.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Key Concepts</div>
    <p>Understanding the difference hinges on how the particles of the medium move as the wave passes through.  Key concepts include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Crest:</b> The highest point of a transverse wave.</li>
      <li><b>Trough:</b> The lowest point of a transverse wave.</li>
      <li><b>Compression:</b> A region in a longitudinal wave where the particles are closest together.</li>
      <li><b>Rarefaction:</b> A region in a longitudinal wave where the particles are furthest apart.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Characteristics</div>
    <p>Here's a table summarizing the key characteristics:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Characteristic</th>
          <th>Transverse Waves</th>
          <th>Longitudinal Waves</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Direction of Oscillation</td>
          <td>Perpendicular to wave propagation</td>
          <td>Parallel to wave propagation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Examples</td>
          <td>Light waves, water waves (approximately), waves on a string</td>
          <td>Sound waves, seismic P-waves</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Features</td>
          <td>Crests and troughs</td>
          <td>Compressions and rarefactions</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="formula-card">
    <div class="formula-card-title">Wave Speed Formula (General)</div>
    <div class="formula-container">
      $$v = f \lambda$$
    </div>
    <div class="button-container">
      <button class="button">Explain Formula</button>
      <button class="button">Open Formula Lab</button>
    </div>
  </div>


  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Applications</div>
    <p><b>Transverse Waves:</b> Used in fiber optics for data transmission (light waves). Understanding water wave behavior is crucial for coastal engineering and navigation.  Seismic S-waves, which are transverse, help us understand the Earth's interior.</p>
    <p><b>Longitudinal Waves:</b> Crucial for understanding sound and how we hear. Used in medical imaging (ultrasound). Seismic P-waves, which are longitudinal, also provide information about the Earth's interior.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Advantages</div>
    <p><b>Transverse Waves:</b> Can travel through solids, liquids, and (under certain conditions) the surface of liquids. Polarization is a key property.</p>
    <p><b>Longitudinal Waves:</b>  Can travel through all states of matter (solids, liquids, and gases).</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Disadvantages</div>
    <p><b>Transverse Waves:</b>  Cannot travel through gases (with the exception of electromagnetic waves like light).  Water waves are only approximately transverse; they have a more complex motion.</p>
    <p><b>Longitudinal Waves:</b>  The speed of sound, a longitudinal wave, is affected by the medium's temperature and density, potentially leading to distortions.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Important Notes</div>
    <p>Electromagnetic waves (light, radio waves, etc.) are always transverse waves. Sound waves are always longitudinal waves. Water waves are a combination of both.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Summary</div>
    <p>Transverse waves and longitudinal waves are fundamental concepts in understanding how energy propagates. The key difference lies in the direction of the medium's oscillation relative to the wave's propagation direction.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Suggested Questions</div>
