          ["Temperature effect", "Linear increase in R", "Complex, non-linear"],
        ]}
      />

      {/* Limitations */}
      <SectionCard icon="⚠️" title="Limitations of Ohm's Law" color="red">
        <p className="text-sm text-gray-600 mb-3">
          Ohm's Law is <span className="font-bold text-red-700">not a universal law</span>. It fails under the following conditions:
        </p>

        <LimitationCard
          number="1"
          title="Non-linear (Non-Ohmic) Devices"
          description="For devices like diodes, transistors, and thyristors, the V-I relationship is not linear. The resistance changes with applied voltage."
          example="In a p-n junction diode, current increases exponentially with voltage, not linearly."
        />
        <LimitationCard
          number="2"
          title="Unidirectional Devices"
          description="Some devices conduct differently in forward and reverse bias. The V-I graph is not symmetric about the origin, violating Ohm's Law."
          example="A semiconductor diode allows current in one direction but blocks it in the other."
        />
        <LimitationCard
          number="3"
          title="Temperature-Sensitive Conductors"
          description="For materials like thermistors and tungsten filaments, resistance changes significantly with temperature. Since Ohm's Law requires constant physical conditions, it breaks down."
          example="The resistance of a tungsten filament increases drastically as it heats up in a bulb."
        />
        <LimitationCard
          number="4"
          title="Electrolytes and Ionized Gases"
