                </div>
                <div class="card-body">
                    <p>A spring-mass system is a classic model used in physics to demonstrate <strong>Simple Harmonic Motion (SHM)</strong>. When a mass attached to a spring is displaced from its equilibrium position, a restoring force pulls it back, causing it to oscillate.</p>
                </div>
            </article>

            <!-- Section: Definition -->
            <article class="card">
                <div class="card-header">
                    <span><i class="icon">📌</i> Quick Definition</span>
                </div>
                <div class="card-body">
                    <h2>Stiffness Constant ($k$)</h2>
                    <p>The <strong>stiffness constant</strong> (or spring constant) denoted by $k$, measures the rigidity of the spring. It defines the relationship between the force applied to the spring and the distance it stretches (Hooke's Law).</p>
                    <p>A higher $k$ value means a stiffer spring that is harder to stretch, while a lower $k$ means a looser spring.</p>
                </div>
            </article>

            <!-- Section: Formula -->
            <article class="card formula-card">
                <div class="card-header">
                    <span><i class="icon">📐</i> Main Formula</span>
                </div>
                <div class="card-body">
                    <p>The period of oscillation ($T$), which is the time taken to complete one full cycle, depends on the mass ($m$) and the stiffness constant ($k$).</p>

                    <div class="formula-display" id="period-formula">
                        <!-- KaTeX rendered here -->
                        $$ T = 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{m}{k}} $$
                    </div>

                    <div class="formula-actions">
                        <button class="btn btn-outline" onclick="showExplanation()">
                            Explain Formula
                        </button>
