                    <span><i class="icon">🔍</i> Analysis</span>
                </div>
                <div class="card-body">
                    <h2>What happens if $k$ is doubled?</h2>
                    <p>Let's substitute $k$ with $2k$ in the period formula:</p>
                    <div style="background: #f8fafc; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 1rem 0; overflow-x: auto;">
                        $$ T_{new} = 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{m}{2k}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \left( 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{m}{k}} \right) $$
                        $$ T_{new} \approx 0.707 \times T_{original} $$
                    </div>
                    <p>When the stiffness constant is doubled, the period of oscillation decreases by a factor of $\sqrt{2}$ (approximately 0.707). This means the spring will oscillate <strong>faster</strong>, completing cycles in less time.</p>
